      \field{abstract}{Sparse autoencoders ({SAEs}) are an unsupervised method for learning a sparse decomposition of a neural network's latent representations into seemingly interpretable features. Despite recent excitement about their potential, research applications outside of industry are limited by the high cost of training a comprehensive suite of {SAEs}. In this work, we introduce Gemma Scope, an open suite of {JumpReLU} {SAEs} trained on all layers and sub-layers of Gemma 2 2B and 9B and select layers of Gemma 2 27B base models. We primarily train {SAEs} on the Gemma 2 pre-trained models, but additionally release {SAEs} trained on instruction-tuned Gemma 2 9B for comparison. We evaluate the quality of each {SAE} on standard metrics and release these results. We hope that by releasing these {SAE} weights, we can help make more ambitious safety and interpretability research easier for the community. Weights and a tutorial can be found at https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-scope and an interactive demo can be found at https://www.neuronpedia.org/gemma-scope}
